# Testing discipline

## Every check must be able to fail

**Every check must be able to fail, and you must be able to name the edit that
makes it fail. Where no check exists, encode the constraint in a type so its
absence cannot be silent.**

This is the negative-control rule you already apply to tests, generalized to
every verification instrument. A gate, a ratchet row, a lint, a retirement
trigger and an assertion are all checks. None of them get the control a test
gets, and the cost of that has been measured.

**The operational test takes ten seconds, at the moment you write the check:**
*name the edit that would make this red.* If you cannot, the check is
decorative. Where it is cheap, do it rather than say it — inject the violation,
watch it refuse, revert.

### Seven instances, one property

Every one of these was green, and none of them could have gone red:

| Instrument | Why it could not fail |
|---|---|
| `if grep -rn PATTERN <paths>` | GNU grep exits **2** on a missing path and `if` cannot tell that from **1** (no match) — a deleted directory silently disabled the gate *including when the pattern was present* |
| A pipeline gate, `grep … \| grep -v …` | a pipeline's status is the LAST command's, so the producer's verdict was discarded and never read |
| An FFI boundary ratchet row | pinned to a file that had been deleted |
| A FOLLOWUPS retirement trigger | never coupled to the call site it named; it fired twice and retired nothing |
| `#![deny(unsafe_code)]` | its subject was inside a `cfg(windows)` arm, compiled away on the machine the lint ran on |
| A `#[test]` | deleted by an over-wide edit; a missing test compiles clean and reports nothing |
| `assert!(!s.contains(<literal SID>))` | Windows canonicalises well-known SIDs to SDDL abbreviations, so the literal never appears and the negation always held |

Six of the seven would have been caught by asking the question once.

### The seventh case: no check at all

The last one is degenerate — you cannot mutation-test a check that does not
exist. A `uds://` transport was selected by `strip_prefix` on a
platform-independent enum variant, so the platform constraint lived in the
*implementation* of the transport rather than in the *type that selects it*,
and the compiler learned of it too late to check the parse surface.

That is where the second half applies: **encode the constraint in a type.**
Gating the variant with `#[cfg(unix)]` turns silence into a build error. The
same move as deleting `wallet2.cpp` rather than documenting that nobody should
extend it.

### What this subsumes

- **Scope by reference set, not by container** — a container-scoped check
  cannot fail once the container empties.
- **A seal is not coverage**; an armed trigger is not a fired one.
- **Observe a new test failing before trusting it** — this is that rule, with
  the word "test" replaced by "check".

### Repetition of a blind method is not coverage

A list confirmed three times by the same method is not three-times confirmed
against a class that method cannot see. A grep of API *usage* cannot contain a
defect whose signature is the *absence* of a check in a string match. Say what
was confirmed and by what instrument, because "confirmed three times" reads as
coverage.


## Property tests for cryptographic correctness

Cryptographic operations have property tests that exercise the algebraic
relations, not just specific values:

- `construct_output → scan_output → recover` round-trips byte-for-byte
- `prove → verify` accepts honest proofs and rejects malformed ones
- `derive_output_secrets` is deterministic for fixed inputs

Property tests run with `proptest::ProptestConfig::with_cases(N)` where:

- N = 10,000 on every PR (CI gate)
- N = 1,000,000 nightly with 30-minute wall-clock ceiling

The wall-clock ceiling failing is itself a regression signal — if iterations
got slower, find out why.

## Test the production code, not a local re-implementation

If a test defines local helpers that mirror production behavior, the test
verifies the helpers, not the production code. Tests that exercise FFI
surfaces must call the real FFI via `unsafe extern`, not a Rust-side
reimplementation.

## Test fixtures are spec-derived, not behavior-derived

A fixture written as "whatever values make the existing test pass" will
satisfy whatever rule the existing code happens to enforce, including a
wrong rule. Fixtures must be derivable from the specification — for tree
depth N, the branch-layer fixture is computed from N according to the
documented tower structure, not observed empirically and pasted in.

When a fixture and the code under test are both wrong in complementary ways,
the test passes but verifies nothing. Surface this with property tests
sampling unfamiliar values, FFI tests calling the production path, or review
by someone who didn't write the code.

## A fixture that constructs the state a rule reads back is not a test of the rule

A mock that is *told* a value and then *serves* it to the code under test
closes a loop with nothing inside it. `MockChain` handed `root_at(40) = R`
and a proof made against `R` passes whether or not `R` is the root of any
tree, whether or not the leaf the spend claims is in it, whether or not the
store would compute `R` at height 40. The test exercises the code path and
proves that the rule reads what it was handed. It is not weaker evidence
about production behaviour; it is **none**, wearing a green check.

Distrust anything named `Mock*` on sight, because the name marks the place
where someone chose to construct the conditions instead of producing them.
A mock earns its keep in exactly three situations, and its doc comment says
which:

1. **Predicate logic on plain values, where there is no state to fake.** A
   window's boundary arithmetic, an ordering comparison, a count. The mock
   is a struct literal with a different name.
2. **Faults the real substrate cannot be made to exhibit on demand.** "The
   rule propagates a store fault as a fault, never a verdict" needs a view
   that fails at height 7; a real store will not.
3. **States a conforming substrate cannot hold — malformity by wrapper.**
   A view whose tip says 12 and whose rows stop at 7; a root missing at a
   height the same view just reported recorded. A conforming store refuses
   to hold these (SI-2, SI-4, SI-7), so no driven chain can produce them,
   and the validator's answer to them — `Corrupt::HoleBelowTip`, the
   controlled fault that replaced four `unreachable!`s — has **no other way
   to be reached by a test**. The instrument is `WithholdingView`: one
   `WithheldRead` (`BlockAt` or `RootAt` at one height) over the dense
   mock, and the assertion is the *classification* — the fault arm, never
   a verdict.

The line between the three and everything else is **whether the chain is
the subject**. I11's window is arithmetic that happens to take heights as
inputs; a wrapper's hole is a shape of view, not a fact about any chain.
"This block exists", "this root is what the tree grew", "this depth is
consistent with the leaves" are assertions *about a chain*, and a
constructed view answering them tests the construction. So the split is
not mock-versus-driver; it is three things — **arithmetic on the mock,
operation on the driver, unreachable-by-construction states on wrappers
over the mock** — and a test that is none of the three is the false test
above. (E6 slice 6 commit 5, 2026-09-25: I10 and I12 operate on the
driver, I11's boundaries on the mock, `HoleBelowTip` on `WithholdingView`.)

Everything else — a computed root, a tree depth, a spent set populated to
make a rule fire, a membership set that exists because a test said so — is
served-as-given state, and the test that consumes it is the false test
above. **The witness for any rule that reads derived state is the real
chain**: captured blocks replayed through the production path against a
real store, so the state the rule reads was *derived* by the code that
derives it in production, and a negative fixture is a mutation of a real
transaction replayed the same way. The dependency-direction belt
(`check_chain_rules_no_store.sh`) forbids the validator crate from owning
that chain; the ingest crate, which depends on both, is where the witness
lives. A rule whose only fixture is mock-served state is **untested for
parity**, whatever its coverage row says.

The instance that named this (E6 slice 6, 2026-09-24): the verification
rows — I15's FCMP++ proof, I18's PQC signature, H19's range proof — were
about to be fixtured against a `MockChain` serving the captured block
context. The mock's twin-chain conformance test (`shekyl-chain-store`
§8.8) is not a counter-example: its subject is the mock's fidelity to the
store's **read API**, not any rule's behaviour.

## Every vector declares its oracle; only external or independent oracles are KATs

A pinned test vector is a claim about *who vouches for the value*. Three
oracle tiers, in descending strength:

1. **External** — the value was produced or accepted by an independent
   implementation (a NIST vector, a published wordlist hash, a live daemon
   accepting a transaction). May be called a KAT.
2. **Independent** — the value is re-derived inside the test from normative
   spec text through a code path that does not share the implementation
   under test (e.g., raw HMAC per RFC 5869 checked against the module's
   HKDF wrapper). May be called a KAT.
3. **Self-pinned** — the value was produced by the module under test and
   frozen. This is a **drift tripwire**: it catches unintentional change,
   but if the module was wrong on the day the value was pinned, the vector
   is wrong with it. It is not a KAT and must not carry the name.

Every pinned vector's declaration site states its tier. The name is part of
the declaration: `kat_*` / `KAT_*` names are reserved for tiers 1–2;
self-pinned vectors use `pinned_*` / a name that says tripwire, not oracle.
The motivating instance is `hkdf_golden_outputs_kat_profile`
(`wallet_envelope.rs`): a self-pinned R1/R2 pair wearing the KAT name, which
read as spec-level assurance it never had.

**Regenerating a self-pinned vector is a decision, not a command.** A
regenerator test that rewrites fixture files on request is a one-command
silencer for a failing tripwire. Regenerators refuse to run unless the
invocation cites the decision-log entry that authorizes the regeneration
(see `pinned_fixtures_regenerate` in `wallet_envelope.rs` for the armed
shape), and the decision-log entry names what moved and why.

## Test names describe properties, not implementations

- `test_amount_zero_round_trip` — good (names the invariant)
- `test_construct_output` — bad (names the function)

A reader scanning test names should be able to enumerate the properties the
system maintains without reading test bodies.

## Test rationales state their coverage boundary, not their association

A test's recorded rationale — the doc line, the discharge-table entry, the
comment a reviewer reads instead of the body — must state **what breaks if
this test is removed**, not **what property the test is associated with**.
The two are not the same, and conflating them is how a leaf-check test gets
credited with proving a mechanism it never exercises.

Write every guard-test's rationale in the necessary-not-sufficient-explicit
form: **"this bites against X; it does NOT cover Y."** The negative half is
load-bearing — it tells the next reviewer the coverage boundary rather than
letting them infer completeness from the association ("this test is about
the exclusion" → "the exclusion is tested").

The motivating instance is the Q11 balance-exclusion KAT
(`tests/unit_tests/archival_emission_ct_balance.cpp`,
`REWARD_EMISSION_E3_GATING_ROUND.md` §2.2). Its recorded rationale claimed an
identity assertion proved the backing-exclusion mechanism; a source read
found the assertion was green-by-construction (the backing is never an input
to the balance function whose verdict is compared) and the test actually
pinned only the *leaf* size check. The rationale described what the test was
*for*, not what it *exercises* — so a leaf-check test was credited with a
dispatch-level proof. The fix was not just correcting that one line but
stating each arm's coverage boundary in the test header and the discharge
table.

This is the same discipline as a tripwire caveat or a cause-blindness note:
**state the boundary of what the guard covers, in the guard's own record**,
so a green test suite is not read as a complete one.

## Diagnostic output: verbose on failure, never leaking secrets

Tests should produce maximum useful diagnostic output when they fail —
developers, not users, read it, and good diagnostics shorten the
hypothesis-to-root-cause loop. But test diagnostics are also a privileged
channel into program state, and these rules are non-negotiable:

- **Never print, log, or `Debug`-format raw secret material.** Secret types
  redact in their `Debug` impl (or don't derive `Debug` at all). The full
  list lives in `35-secure-memory.mdc`.
- **Plant canaries on error paths and assert their absence in error output.**
  The "no 64-char hex in error messages" heuristic is a supplement, not a
  replacement for explicit canary checks.
- **Prefer structured failure messages over scattered prints.** Use
  `proptest`'s shrinking output, `assert_eq!` with descriptive messages, or
  `eprintln!` gated on the failure branch — not unconditional `println!` on
  the happy path.
- **`dbg!` is fine in development, never committed.** CI rejects `dbg!` in
  merged code.

If a test would benefit from emitting a value that *might* be secret-derived,
treat that as a signal that the type is missing a redacted `Debug` and fix
the type, not the test.

## Audit tables in PR descriptions

For changes that touch many sites (>10), the PR description includes:

| Site | File:Line | Disposition | Rationale |
|------|-----------|-------------|-----------|
| ...  | ...       | correct/fixed/deleted | ... |

The audit is done when every row has a disposition and the count matches the
grep count. "Audit confirmed" without evidence is unfalsifiable.
